// Bans every reference to `createRouterTransport` from `@connectrpc/connect`
// in package sources outside the two files allowed by `biome.json`.
//
// Why: a router transport built directly over a service router dispatches
// calls straight to the handlers. It does not run the interceptors that
// `createServer({ interceptors })` configures, so authentication,
// authorization and validation would silently not apply to such calls.
// `createLocalTransport` in `@connectum/core` is the one place that builds
// the in-process transport, and it wires the server interceptor chain in.
//
// The pattern matches the identifier itself, so it catches a named import,
// a direct call, a namespace member call (`connect.createRouterTransport`)
// and a dynamic-import member (`(await import(...)).createRouterTransport`).
// An aliased import (`createRouterTransport as x`) is a separate syntax node
// and needs its own branch. Comments and string literals are not matched.
//
// Scope lives in the `includes` of the plugin entry in `biome.json`. Those
// globs must start with `**/`: with Biome 2.5 a glob such as
// `packages/*/src/**` never matched, neither from the repository root nor
// from a package directory, so the rule silently ran nowhere.
// `lint/no-direct-router-transport.test.mjs` pins that the rule fires.
language js

or {
    `createRouterTransport as $alias`,
    `createRouterTransport`,
    `$object["createRouterTransport"]`
} as $hit where {
    register_diagnostic(span=$hit, message="Do not use createRouterTransport directly: it bypasses the server interceptor chain (authentication, authorization, validation). Use createLocalTransport from @connectum/core.", severity="error")
}
